# XSH assert()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# A failing assert() aborts the process, so every failing case runs in a
# forked child whose standard error (and, where relevant, standard output)
# goes to a pipe the parent reads; the parent reaps the child. The exact
# wording of the message is not specified, only what it must include.

assertion  XSH.assert.001
anchor     functions/assert.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       assert() with a true expression (an int, a pointer, a floating
           value, a long) does nothing visible and execution continues; the
           expression is evaluated.
test       t/001-true.c

assertion  XSH.assert.002
anchor     functions/assert.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       assert() with a false expression calls abort(): a child with the
           default SIGABRT action dies of SIGABRT, and a child that catches
           SIGABRT sees its handler run.
test       t/002-aborts.c

assertion  XSH.assert.003
anchor     functions/assert.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       The message a failed assert() writes goes to standard error (and
           nothing to standard output) and contains the text of the
           expression, the file name given by __FILE__, the line number given
           by __LINE__ and the name of the enclosing function.
test       t/003-message.c

assertion  XSH.assert.004
anchor     functions/assert.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       When NDEBUG is defined before <assert.h> is included, assert()
           has no effect: a false expression does not abort and is not
           evaluated.
test       t/004-ndebug.c

assertion  XSH.assert.005
anchor     functions/assert.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       The expression may have any scalar type: a null pointer, 0.0 and
           0L are false (abort), while a non-null pointer, 0.5 and -1L are
           true.
test       t/005-scalar-types.c
